Spec: ProjectGraph

Version:
  1

Purpose:
  Graph layer that maintains what the project is related to, who owns
   what, and which impact areas a change will cover. This directory is
   only a semantic map — it is not a second copy of the source code.
   AI uses this graph to resolve without traversing the repository.

Owns: ./

Types:
  DependencyGraph  — "What is A dependent on?"
  ImpactGraph      — "What is affected when A changes?"
  OwnershipGraph   — "Who is responsible for this work?"


Graph:
  Nodes:
    @users
    @payments
    @payments/refund
    @api/refund
    @db/refunds
    @FE/payments
    @MD/payments
    @QA/payments
    @SC/payment
    @DO/payment

  Edges:
    @payments/refund -> @db/refunds
      type: dependency
      risk: R3
      required: true

    @payments/refund -> @api/refund
      type: dependency
      risk: R3
      required: true

    @payments/refund -> @payment-gateway
      type: dependency
      risk: R2
      required: true

    @api/refund -> @FE/payments
      type: calls
      protocol: HTTP
      contract: @api/refund

    @api/refund -> @MD/payments
      type: calls
      protocol: HTTP
      contract: @api/refund

    @FE/payments -> @QA/payments
      type: tests
      framework: playwright

    @MD/payments -> @QA/payments
      type: tests
      framework: detox

    @QA/payments -> @SC/payment
      type: security-review
      required: true

    @SC/payment -> @DO/payment
      type: deploys
      required: true

    @DO/payment -> @VR
      type: verifies
      required: true

  Ownership:
    @payments/refund: BE
    @api/refund: API
    @FE/payments: FE
    @MD/payments: MD
    @QA/payments: QA
    @SC/payment: SC
    @DO/payment: DO


DependencyGraph:
  Answers the question "What is A dependent on?"

  Example:
    @payments/refund
      ├──> @db/refunds
      ├──> @api/refund
      └──> @payment-gateway

  AI MUST load this graph before touching any node's implementation
   so it knows what must exist first (see TokenEconomy in ../architecture/
   architecture.sdd -> Principles).


ImpactGraph:
  Answers the question "What is affected when A changes?"

  Example:
    @db/refunds
      │
      ↓
     @BE/payments/refund
      │
      ↓
     @api/refund
      │
      ┌────┴────┐
      ↓         ↓
     @FE        @MD
      │         │
      └────┬────┘
           ↓
          @TS
           ↓
          @QA
           ↓
          @VR

  Impact levels (from protocol/ROOT.sdd -> ImpactTokens):
    I0 = isolated
    I1 = local
    I2 = module
    I3 = domain
    I4 = project
    I5 = system

  AI MUST consult this graph before claiming a change is complete —
  "BE ✓" does not mean DONE while FE, MD, QA, SC, DO, VR are still
  pending (bax chains/chains.sdd -> Completion).


OwnershipGraph:
  Answers the question "Who is responsible for this work?"

  Example:
    @payments/refund   owner: BE
    @api/refund        owner: API
    @FE/payments       owner: FE
    @MD/payments       owner: MD
    @QA/payments       owner: QA
    @SC/payment        owner: SC
    @DO/payment        owner: DO

  This graph is the basis for failure routing (bax tasks/tasks.sdd ->
  FailureRouting, protocol/ROOT.sdd -> FailureRouting).


EdgeMetadata:
  Every graph edge MAY carry metadata:

    type:        dependency | calls | uses | writes | tests |
                 secures | deploys | verifies | produces | consumes
    protocol:    HTTP | gRPC | CLI | Event | ...
    contract:    @api/refund
    risk:        R0-R5
    required:    true | false
    direction:   inbound | outbound
    since:       <version or date>
    status:      + | ~ | ! | ?

  Edge metadata makes the graph usable for architecture intelligence,
  not just navigation. AI MUST NOT create edges without metadata when
  the information is available (bax architecture/architecture.sdd ->
  SourceOfTruth).


GraphDrift:
  Graph drift is a subset of project drift (bax project/project.sdd ->
  Declared/Observed/Drift). When the graph says:

    @payments/refund -> @db/refunds

  but the actual code has:

    @payments/refund -> @db/refunds
    @payments/refund -> @redis/cache

  AI MUST:
    1. Detect the drift
    2. Create a DRIFT finding
    3. Create a task to reconcile the graph
    4. NOT silently update the graph without human approval for
       significant changes (bax PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision)

  Drift severity:
    D0 = no drift
    D1 = minor (new optional dependency)
    D2 = significant (missing dependency, wrong owner)
    D3 = critical (security gap, architecture violation)


FallbackDiscovery:
  Graph is the primary source of truth for relationships. When the
  graph says nothing about a node, AI MUST fall back to discovery:

    GRAPH MISSING
      ↓
    DISCOVER
      ↓
    OBSERVE
      ↓
    DRAFT UPDATE
      ↓
    HUMAN APPROVAL (if significant)
      ↓
    UPDATE GRAPH

  AI MUST NOT silently update the global graph. Domain-local graph
  updates MAY be proposed automatically, but MUST be reviewed before
  being accepted (bax HumanDecision in PROJECT.sdd).


DomainGraph:
  Each domain MAY have its own local graph:

    .sdd/project/payments/graph.sdd

  containing only the nodes and edges relevant to that domain. The
  global graph at .sdd/graph/graph.sdd contains only high-level
  domain-to-domain relationships.

  Resolution order:
    1. Global graph (.sdd/graph/graph.sdd)
    2. Domain graph (.sdd/project/<domain>/graph.sdd)
    3. Component graph (.sdd/project/<domain>/<component>.sdd)
    4. Fallback discovery (if all graphs are silent)

  This keeps the global graph small and the domain graph detailed —
  context locality (bax TokenEconomy in architecture/architecture.sdd
  -> Principles).


Navigation:
  AI SHOULD resolve, top to bottom, before touching source code:

    INDEX.sdd
      -> PROJECT.sdd
        -> project/PROJECT.sdd
          -> domain graph
            -> component graph
              -> source code

  This is the primary resolution path. AI MUST NOT skip to source
  code without first resolving the graph (bax TokenEconomy in
  architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles).

State: +
  Note:
  This file is the entry point for the
  project graph layer. It does not duplicate content from project/
  project.sdd — it references the project model and provides the
  relationship layer on top of it. The source proposed splitting the
  graph into dependencies.sdd, ownership.sdd, impact.sdd, api.sdd,
  db.sdd; that structure is noted in the Types section above but a
  single graph.sdd is kept as the authoritative entry point to avoid
  fragmentation at this phase (NoDuplication, skeleton + rule engine
  precedent). Domain-level graph files are created once real project
  analysis justifies them (bax ./instantiation.sdd -> CorePrinciple,
  "PROJECT IS GENERATED, NOT PREDEFINED").
